Hadal Gloss
Ingredients: LIQUID FUNK + J-POP + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Slide Guitar
Creative Catalyst: The sound was discovered by commercial deep-sea divers repairing transatlantic data cables who intercepted acoustic packet bleed ringing through subsea repeater valves.

Hadal Gloss is an intoxicating, high-velocity collision of 174-BPM liquid drum and bass rollers, effervescent Shibuya-kei pop hooks, and drifting, lysergic atmospheres. At its core, weeping Hawaiian lap steel and glass-bottleneck slide guitars are drenched in cavernous multi-tap delays, suspended miraculously above warm rolling 808 sub-bass, chirpy Japanese idol vocal harmonies, and disorienting underwater psychoacoustics.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 170–175 BPM. The pulse relies on velvety, syncopated liquid two-step breaks and light amen shuffles that glide smoothly beneath syncopated, rubbery sub-bass grooves.
- Harmonic Palette: Major 7th, 9th, and minor 11th jazz chords straight out of classic 1990s Japanese pop, crossed with microtonal blues glissandos and pitch-drifting, open-tuned Hawaiian slacks that slide between western scales.
- Production Signatures: 1) Benthic Reverb Filtering: Lap steel slides processed through pitch-shifted reverse delays that simulate extreme oceanic depth and pressure; 2) Chirality Vocals: Dual-tracked J-pop vocal runs hard-panned and sidechained heavily to the sub-bass, producing an airy, breathless suction effect; 3) Hydro-Chamber Flanging: Slow tape-phasing across the master bus that makes the track feel as though it is breaching water surfaces between drops.
- Dynamics: Songs open in deep-sea sensory deprivation—distant sonar pings, tape warble, and lone slide guitar notes crying in isolation—before suddenly detonating into euphoric, sunlit drum-and-bass cadences with candy-sweet vocal hooks, cycling through zero-gravity ambient dropouts.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement materialized between the damp shipping docks of Halifax and the neon-streaked walk-ups of Tokyo's Oji district in the late 2010s. Commercial hydro-telecom divers returning from weeks on North Atlantic cable-laying vessels brought back DAT cassettes of bizarre acoustic crosstalk—radio signals from East Asia that had mechanically coupled into optical fiber armor miles beneath the surface. Sound nerds in Tokyo began looping these ghostly, pressure-distorted pop fragments over crisp liquid funk rollers, recruiting Hawaiian slack-key and blues slide guitarists to mimic the uncanny frequency bends of deep-water hydrophones. The subculture quickly claimed city roofs: fans known as Kaiteizen ("Trench Dwellers") gather at night atop high-density apartment blocks with cheap tripod telescopes and shortwave radio receivers, wearing high-visibility neoprene diving vests over floral vintage rayon shirts, sipping cold canned matcha, and dancing in tight, gliding footwork to music that treats the sky like an inverted ocean.

Typical Instruments: Open-tuned electric lap steel and 1930s National resonator guitars (played with glass or brass slides), Roland JD-800 and Yamaha DX7 digital synthesizers, Akai S950 hardware samplers for chopped breaks, Fender Precision Bass doubled with pure 808 sub-sine sweeps, pitched high-register vocals, and spring-reverb tanks.

For Fans Of: Makoto, Fishmans, Calibre, Cornelius

